Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 22:06:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] net/mlx5e: ethtool l4_4_bytes steering via the flex parser

ethtool's IP_USER_FLOW and IPV6_USER_FLOW specs can match the first four
bytes after the IP header (l4_4_bytes, "l4data" on the command line). For
an IP protocol the NIC does not parse, that is the only way to steer by
the protocol's own header: RSS hashes only the IP addresses, so all of its
traffic between two hosts lands on one RX queue.

mlx5e rejects l4_4_bytes. Devices with a programmable flex parser can
match it through a parse graph node. Patch 1 adds the node's PRM layout,
patch 2 uses it for IPv4 rules and patch 3 extends it to IPv6.

The node is tracked per netdev, which assumes one netdev per function;
functions with several netdevs (switchdev representors, IPoIB child
interfaces) are not handled.

Tested on BlueField-3 (firmware 32.47.3576, NV PROG_PARSE_GRAPH=1 and
FLEX_PARSER_PROFILE_ENABLE=4) with bare IPv4 and IPv6 packets of protocol
253 whose first dword is a port pair:

- 16 rules on the low nibble of the destination port spread 160000
  packets as exactly 10000 per queue, for IPv4 and for IPv6. Without
  rules all of them hit one queue and 205-3356 were dropped for lack of
  RX buffers (three runs).
- IPv4 and IPv6 rules for one protocol share the node; a rule for a
  second protocol fails with -EBUSY.
- l4_4_bytes fails with -EINVAL for the refused protocols, and with
  -EOPNOTSUPP on a ConnectX-4 Lx, which lacks the capability.
- With the node in place, UDP keeps its RSS spread and udp4 rules work.
- 50 add/delete cycles, and module removal with rules installed, are
  clean.

Based on v6.18.55.

